/T (In PyTorch, when you call loss.backward\(\) multiple times, the new gradient is not overwritten in the .grad property, but is added to the existing value \(accumulated\). This is the intended behavior for certain models, such as RNNs, but in most cases, you want to compute a new gradient for every training step \(epoch or iteration\).)
/E (In PyTorch, when you call loss.backward\(\) multiple times, the new gradient is not overwritten in the .grad property, but is added to the existing value \(accumulated\). This is the intended behavior for certain models, such as RNNs, but in most cases, you want to compute a new gradient for every training step \(epoch or iteration\).)
/Alt ()
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [0 1]
>>
